public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        List<Id> matchedContactIds = new List<Id>();
        if (leads == null || leads.isEmpty()) {
            return matchedContactIds;
        }

        Map<String, Id> emailToContactId = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact c : contacts) {
                if (c != null && String.isNotBlank(c.Email)) {
                    String normalizedEmail = c.Email.trim().toLowerCase();
                    if (!emailToContactId.containsKey(normalizedEmail)) {
                        emailToContactId.put(normalizedEmail, c.Id);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        for (Lead l : leads) {
            if (l != null && String.isNotBlank(l.Email)) {
                String normalizedEmail = l.Email.trim().toLowerCase();
                matchedContactIds.add(emailToContactId.get(normalizedEmail));
            } else {
                matchedContactIds.add(null);
            }
        }

        return matchedContactIds;
    }
}
```

### Explanation of Changes

1. **Bulkified Queueable Enqueuing (Governor Limit Fix):**
   The original design asked to loop over newly closed cases in the trigger and call `System.enqueueJob(new CaseSurveyJob(c.Id))` once per case. In Salesforce, a synchronous transaction can enqueue at most **50 Queueable jobs** (`Limits.getLimitQueueableJobs()`), and an asynchronous transaction can enqueue at most **1**. Because agents close cases in bulk from list views and a nightly integration closes up to **200 cases at a time**, enqueuing one job per case would fail with `System.LimitException: Too many queueable jobs added to the queue: 51`.
   - **Change:** `CaseTrigger` collects all qualifying Case IDs (`!oldCase.IsClosed && newCase.IsClosed && newCase.ContactId != null`) into a `Set<Id>` and enqueues **a single** `CaseSurveyJob` for the entire trigger batch.
   - `CaseSurveyJob` accepts a `Set<Id>` (as well as overloaded `List<Id>` and single `Id` constructors for flexibility/backward compatibility).

2. **Partial Success for Fault Isolation (`allOrNone = false`):**
   To preserve the requirement that a failure on one case must not affect the others within the batch, `CaseSurveyJob` inserts the `Task` records using `Database.insert(tasks, false)`. If one task fails validation or DML rules, the remaining tasks in the job still succeed.

3. **Re-verification in Asynchronous Context:**
   `CaseSurveyJob` queries the cases (`WHERE Id IN :this.caseIds AND IsClosed = true AND ContactId != null`) when the job executes to retrieve `CaseNumber` and `ContactId`, ensuring that only cases that still have a contact and remain closed receive a follow-up survey task.

